 a response, so the "best zones" are adjacent to H4. H5 is adjacent and towards the center of the board, so that is the solid choice. I\'m choosing H5 as it directly blocks a cardinal direction. Let\'s go with H5.\n\nSince H4 is somewhat non-standard (not center H8), playing H5 helps me gain spatial influence near the center. H4 is the only stone on the board, and H5 is empty. Let\'s stick with H5. It is close enough to the center. So, my final answer should be {"col": "H", "row": 5}.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The game is in the opening phase with only one black stone placed at H4. Since there are no immediate threats (such as 3-in-a-row or 4-in-a-row), the strategy is to play a positional move that restricts the opponent\'s development while aiming for the center of the board. The most effective squares are those immediately adjacent to the opponent\'s stone.\n\nReason: Playing at H5 is a solid response. It is immediately adjacent to the black stone at H4, blocking the vertical line development towards the center of the board. By placing a stone at H5, White prevents Black from easily forming a continuous vertical chain and establishes a presence closer to the board\'s center (H8), which is advantageous for future expansion and connecting lines in multiple directions.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "H", "row": 5}'}
